592186045417] [17592186216591 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216591 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216591 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216591 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216591 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8411272a-b29f-5b62-85b4-24db9b8399d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216592 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216592 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216592 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216592 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186495617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216592 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e908cf01-0b05-5721-8a0d-853b457b6c59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216593 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216593 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216593 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216593 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186496012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216593 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe11665f-a742-5b8a-ac1c-8d9534f7c414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216594 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216594 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216594 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216594 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186495837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216594 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "011c8996-2157-52f6-9093-6078e9a4fedc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216999 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216999 #edb/ref 1071 "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times10^{30})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and Mars has a mass of $(6.42\\times10^{23})\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of the Sun and Mars is $(2.28\\times10^{11})\\,\\textrm{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216999 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f37e7ffd-df2d-51a4-9eee-e53589011f6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216999 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of the Sun and $m_2$ be the mass of Mars. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (6.42\\times {10}^{23})}{(2.28\\times {10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 6.42}{(2.28)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{23}}{({10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =(16.392\\,5…)\\times {10}^{20} \\\\  & =(1.639\\,25…)\\times {10}^{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars is\n$\\Big(1.64 \\times 10^{21}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{N}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217000 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21745" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217000 #edb/ref 1071 "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(3.0\\times10^3)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(3.0\\times10^{-4})\\,\\textrm{N}.$ If one object has mass $(2.0\\times10^5)\\,\\textrm{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217000 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ef6ba7e-4f1d-51d2-af64-e1a77a7d748f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217000 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nLet $m_2$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $m_2$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(3.0\\times {10}^{-4})\\cdot (3.0\\times {10}^{3})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (2.0\\times {10}^{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.0\\cdot (3.0)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 2.0}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-4}\\cdot ({10}^{3})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}} \\\\  & =(2.023…)\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$\\Big(2.0 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{kg}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217001 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217001 #edb/ref 1071 "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times10^{24})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and the Moon has a mass of $(7.35\\times10^{22})\\,\\textrm{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.98\\times10^{20})\\,\\textrm{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of Earth and the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217001 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7043d87-7f25-55a3-bd36-49d4ba1ae3fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217001 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of Earth and $m_2$ be the mass of the Moon. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\cdot (7.35\\times {10}^{22})}{1.98\\times {10}^{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 5.97\\cdot 7.35}{1.98}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}\\cdot {10}^{22}}{{10}^{20}} \\\\  & =(147.816…)\\times {10}^{15} \\\\  & =(14.7816…)\\times {10}^{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{14.7816…\\times {10}^{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14.7816…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{16}} \\\\  & =(3.8446…)\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between Earth and the Moon is\n$\\Big(3.84 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186217786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186217787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Gravitational Force Between Two Objects" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186216999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217235 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "61c3724b-7fda-5547-b0cb-7bf9136fc4a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217236 #ed