7592186045417] [17592186217786 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f08a74f-5dc1-59fb-934d-192510b18902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217786 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(7.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(2.0\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(7.5\\times {10}^{5})\\cdot (2.0\\times {10}^{6})}{(4.0\\times {10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 7.5\\cdot 2.0}{(4.0)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{({10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =6.253\\,125\\times {10}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects is\n$(\\,6.3\\times {10}^{-4}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217789 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217789 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217789 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217789 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217789 #edb/ref 1071 "The average distance between the centers of Earth and the Moon is $(3.84\\times {10}^{8})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.98\\times {10}^{20})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of Earth is $(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of the Moon is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217789 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40755d2c-612a-5ccf-a761-10556a446e0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217789 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Earth and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of the Moon.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(1.98\\times {10}^{20})\\cdot (3.84\\times {10}^{8})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (5.97\\times {10}^{24})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.98\\cdot (3.84)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 5.97}\\times \\frac{{10}^{20}\\cdot ({10}^{8})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}} \\\\  & =(0.733\\,2…)\\times {10}^{23} \\\\  & =(7.332…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of the Moon is\n$(\\,7.33\\times {10}^{22}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217790 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217790 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217790 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335148" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217790 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217790 #edb/ref 1071 "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(6.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(9.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(1.9\\times {10}^{-3})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217790 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "141575dc-d270-56a4-9e5c-9b5ce1733ede" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217790 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(6.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(9.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(6.2\\times {10}^{6})\\cdot (9.5\\times {10}^{5})}{1.9\\times {10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 6.2\\cdot 9.5}{1.9}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}\\cdot {10}^{5}}{{10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =206.77\\times {10}^{3} \\\\  & =20.677\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{20.677\\times {10}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20.677}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{4}} \\\\  & =(4.547…)\\times {10}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,4.5\\times {10}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218180 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186218181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218180 #edb/ref 1061 587393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218180 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "830fbba9-b0a4-5f52-82dc-b497af4a9670" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218180 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186218354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218181 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186218182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218181 #edb/ref 1061 587395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218181 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d1d1c724-94a7-5a92-995f-3894e6b8d209" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218181 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186217235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218182 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186218183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218182 #edb/ref 1061 587398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218182 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d4c641d7-2d3c-5680-b0a5-fc7bad179aed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218182 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186217236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218183 #edb/ref 1061 587401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218183 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fefcc436-d1af-5ad3-97cf-682059474c51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218183 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186217237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218354 #edb/ref 1064 "Gravitation is the attractive force that acts between *any two objects with mass*. This means that every pair of objects in the universe is pulling on each other, even if the effect is too small to notice in everyday life.\n\nIn many situations (especially when objects are far apart compared to their sizes), we model each object as if *all of its mass were concentrated at its center of mass*.\nThen the gravitational force depends only on the two masses and the distance between their centers of mass.\n\n![](images/dd/dd462f94b3a49b33ff44cf0868975f2ae902145a9664ce307a397fcc2a2ea93a.png)\n\n**Newton's law of universal gravitation** states that the gravitational force attracting two objects with masses is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons ($\\textrm{N}$),\n\n- $G \\approx 6.674\\times10^{-11}\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian **constant of gravitation**,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nThe force acts along the line connecting the two centers of mass, and each object pulls on the other with the same magnitude.\n\nNewton's law shows two key relationships:\n\n- *More mass means more force:* If either mass increases, the gravitational force increases proportionally.\n\n- *Greater distance means less force:* The force decreases with the square of the distance. So, for example, doubling the distance makes the force one quarter as large.\n\nLet's see some examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218354 #edb/ref 1065 14552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218354 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "135ab76f-4555-55f3-97ed-0c3e76990bc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218354 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308759 #edb/ref 1080 "6.42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308759 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308759 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01b78827-bc2a-5019-be53-c37837890ae7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308811 #edb/ref 1080 "{10}^{23}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308811 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308811 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "085a39b8-34b4-59be-bf78-3ad89c9cee72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309166 #edb/ref 1080 "{10}^{-4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309166 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309166 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "36ff45d5-e2a8-553c-8663-3825a63e87a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309961 #edb/ref 1080 "1.08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309961 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309961 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "97659834-b505-529e-8bad-43eb98c2047b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310149 #edb/ref 1080 "3.2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310149 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310149 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ad602a92-a4e1-55b3-a5f2-ad4a71149f37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310172 #edb/ref 1080 "{10}^{11}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310172 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310172 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b0658bcd-109d-5074-ae2f-ffd4d820d520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310278 #edb/ref 1080 "4.50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310278 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/re