1068 #edb/ref 17592186311446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311673 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311673 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311673 #edb/ref 1071 "The centers of two objects of masses $(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(4.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ are separated by a distance of $(7.5\\times {10}^{2})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311673 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fdaf854a-23e0-590a-afed-80c02813eae0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311673 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(4.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\cdot (4.2\\times {10}^{6})}{(7.5\\times {10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 6.5\\cdot 4.2}{(7.5)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{({10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =3.237\\,173…\\times {10}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects is\n$(\\,3.2\\times {10}^{-4}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311680 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311680 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311680 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336295" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311680 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311680 #edb/ref 1071 "The average distance between the centers of the Sun and Mars is $(2.28\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.64\\times {10}^{21})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of the Sun is $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of Mars is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311680 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6fc2d2a-8149-567b-ab06-063d27b8e5bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311680 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Mars.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(1.64\\times {10}^{21})\\cdot (2.28\\times {10}^{11})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (1.99\\times {10}^{30})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.64\\cdot (2.28)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 1.99}\\times \\frac{{10}^{21}\\cdot ({10}^{11})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}} \\\\  & =(0.642\\,29…)\\times {10}^{24} \\\\  & =(6.4229…)\\times {10}^{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of Mars is\n$(\\,6.42\\times {10}^{23}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311686 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311686 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311686 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336307" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311686 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311686 #edb/ref 1071 "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Neptune has a mass of $(1.02\\times {10}^{26})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(6.69\\times {10}^{20})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the Sun and Neptune? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the Sun and Neptune is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311686 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4734863f-a900-55bb-995a-471d13e8ef2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311686 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Neptune. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (1.02\\times {10}^{26})}{6.69\\times {10}^{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 1.02}{6.69}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{26}}{{10}^{20}} \\\\  & =(2.0237…)\\times {10}^{25} \\\\  & =(20.237…)\\times {10}^{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{20.237…\\times {10}^{24}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20.237…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{24}} \\\\  & =(4.498…)\\times {10}^{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between the Sun and Neptune is\n$(\\,4.50\\times {10}^{12}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311688 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311688 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311688 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336314" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311688 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311688 #edb/ref 1071 "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Venus has a mass of $(4.87\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(5.54\\times {10}^{22})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the Sun and Venus? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the Sun and Venus is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311688 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ad559c9-9cfd-5c87-86a0-09ca886a809d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311688 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Venus. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (4.87\\times {10}^{24})}{5.54\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 4.87}{5.54}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{24}}{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(11.668…)\\times {10}^{21} \\\\  & =(1.1668…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{1.1668…\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.1668…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(1.0801…)\\times {10}^{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between the Sun and Venus is\n$(\\,1.08\\times {10}^{11}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495539 #edb/ref 1080 "4.17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495539 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495539 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b56e2ef-3ed6-5e05-80da-4f4b6f83b676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495546 #edb/ref 1080 "10^{23}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495546 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495546 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ea92853-8daa-5326-97ac-7b18c50fa0c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495553 #edb/ref 1080 "5.3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495553 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495553 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1363f813-3e50-54e2-b909-e2d21c495b1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495585 #edb/ref 1080 "10^{-4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495585 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495585 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2273967a-61db-54d4-b614-08715aac5263" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495617 #edb/ref