/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496224 #edb/ref 1071 "The average distance between the centers of Earth and Jupiter is $(6.29\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.91\\times {10}^{18})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of Earth is $(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of Jupiter? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of Jupiter is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496224 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "047c28c1-e17d-56d3-9e40-ea3f7e7b2867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496224 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Earth and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Jupiter.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(1.91\\times {10}^{18})\\cdot (6.29\\times {10}^{11})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (5.97\\times {10}^{24})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.91\\cdot (6.29)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 5.97}\\times \\frac{{10}^{18}\\cdot ({10}^{11})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}} \\\\  & =(1.8977…)\\times {10}^{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of Jupiter is\n$(\\,1.90\\times {10}^{27}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496229 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496229 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496229 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496229 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496229 #edb/ref 1071 "The average distance between the centers of the Sun and Earth is $(1.50\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(3.52\\times {10}^{22})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of the Sun is $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of Earth? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of Earth is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496229 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10fc12bd-51f2-5325-ac89-b383736a7b6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496229 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Earth.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(3.52\\times {10}^{22})\\cdot (1.50\\times {10}^{11})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (1.99\\times {10}^{30})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.52\\cdot (1.50)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 1.99}\\times \\frac{{10}^{22}\\cdot ({10}^{11})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}} \\\\  & =(0.596\\,68…)\\times {10}^{25} \\\\  & =(5.9668…)\\times {10}^{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of Earth is\n$(\\,5.97\\times {10}^{24}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496241 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496241 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496241 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496241 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496241 #edb/ref 1071 "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Mars has a mass of $(6.42\\times {10}^{23})\\,\\text{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of Earth and Mars is $(7.83\\times {10}^{10})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between Earth and Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between Earth and Mars is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496241 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "36c5d3a3-e17d-52d4-a3d2-5e187ff26b13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496241 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Earth and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Mars. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\cdot (6.42\\times {10}^{23})}{(7.83\\times {10}^{10})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 5.97\\cdot 6.42}{(7.83)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}\\cdot {10}^{23}}{({10}^{10})^{2}} \\\\  & =(4.169\\,76…)\\times {10}^{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between Earth and Mars is\n$(\\,4.17\\times {10}^{16}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496242 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496242 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496242 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336310" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496242 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496242 #edb/ref 1071 "Mars has a mass of $(6.42\\times {10}^{23})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Jupiter has a mass of $(1.90\\times {10}^{27})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(2.69\\times {10}^{17})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of Mars and Jupiter? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between Mars and Jupiter is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496242 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "39855b88-dc8a-571a-b435-84e158ee3c93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496242 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Mars and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Jupiter. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(6.42\\times {10}^{23})\\cdot (1.90\\times {10}^{27})}{2.69\\times {10}^{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 6.42\\cdot 1.90}{2.69}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{23}\\cdot {10}^{27}}{{10}^{17}} \\\\  & =(30.245…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{30.245…\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30.245…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(5.499…)\\times {10}^{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between Mars and Jupiter is\n$(\\,5.50\\times {10}^{11}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496251 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496251 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496251 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496251 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496251 #edb/ref 1071 "The centers of two objects of masses $(8.0\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(2.5\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ are separated by a distance of $(5.0\\times {10}^{2})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496251 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "493d75d3-43a5-5a86-a486-dc06c6727b87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496251 #edb/ref 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(8.0\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(2.5\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(8.0\\times {10}^{4})\\cdot (2.5\\times {10}^{7})}{(5.0\\times {10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 8.0\\cdot 2.5}{(5.0)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{4}\\cdot {10}^{7}}{({10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =5.336\\times {10}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects is\n$(\\,5.3\\times {10}^{-4}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
